  question: "Why does the compiler report a problem with the name 'cat'?"
  answerA: "The name 'cat' is a reserved keyword in C."
  answerB: "A variable name must contain at least four letters."
  answerC: "The name 'cat' must be enclosed in quotation marks."
  answerD: "The variable 'cat' has not been declared before use."
  correctAnswer: 3
  rawFeedback: "name_resolution.c: In function ‘main’:\nname_resolution.c:3:5: error: ‘cat’ undeclared (first use in this function)\n    3 |     cat = \"meow\";\n      |     ^~~\nname_resolution.c:3:5: note: each undeclared identifier is reported only once for each function it appears in"
  neutralFeedback: "name_resolution.c:3:5: error: The name 'cat' is being used without being declared first. Check that the variable has been declared before this statement tries to use it.\n    3 |     cat = \"meow\";\n      |     ^~~"
  meowraFeedback: "name_resolution.c:3:5: error: Hmm, 'cat' is being used without being declared first, nya. Check that the variable has been declared before this statement tries to use it.\n    3 |     cat = \"meow\";\n      |     ^~~"
